    "chapter_name": "Work Power Energy",
    "year": "2022",
    "shift": "shift_2"
  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A bullet of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> having initial kinetic energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math> is shot inside a long swimming pool as shown in the figure. If it&#39;s kinetic energy reduces&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>, the minimum length of the pool, the bullet has&nbsp;to travel so that it completely comes to rest is</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0065_7cf30a36e88d.png\" style=\"width: 160px; height: 89px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>125</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Let us assume that the motion of the bullet is of constant retardation inside the water.</p><p>Initial kinetic energy&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>Let the retardation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>,&nbsp;then after&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>Stopping distance for the constant retardation is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>30</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>20</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>.</p><p>Therefore, the minimum length of the pool required is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}